# AN OBJECT MAP: a model that changes the TYPES, not just the words.
#
# A model is one construct — a presentation interpreted in a category,
# given by an OBJECT MAP and an image for each generator — and until
# now every model in Braid carried the identity object map and wrote
# only the images.  `model Opt in Base = dupInt = dup` renames
# generators; the wires keep their types.  This file writes the other
# half.
#
#     model Mod in Base(Int ↦ Mod7 via reduce) = + = …, * = …
#
# `Int ↦ Mod7` is the map on OBJECTS: the substitution `Int := Mod7`,
# applied structurally — under `List`, under a data type's arguments,
# through an `Fn` arrow, along a stack.  It is a substitution and not a
# type-level function, which is why it is principal: substitution
# commutes with unification, so a transported program has a principal
# type for the same reason the original did.
#
# `via reduce` is the image of the LITERAL FAMILY.  Every literal of
# the mapped type is a generator — there are infinitely many of them
# and none of them is a word — so a table cannot name them and the head
# does: under `with Mod`, `3` becomes `3 ; reduce`.
#
# Everything else is the table, and the table may name ANY WORD, because
# `Base`'s generators are every word in scope.  What makes this a stage
# rather than a bigger table is the third rule: a word that MENTIONS the
# mapped type and has no image is UNFOLDED — F(def) = F(body) —
# transitively, and cached.  A word whose type never mentions `Int` is
# left exactly as it was, because the functor is the identity off `Int`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The carrier, and the map on values
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Mod7` is Z/7 by canonical representatives: the nominal type says
# which Ints are meant, and `reduce` is the map that means them.
data Mod7 = Int

def reduce = (x -> x 7 ; mod ; Mod7)

# `mod` is Euclidean here, so the representative is always in 0..6 —
# including for a negative argument (-3 mod 7 = 4).
-3 7 ; mod ; print                                    # 4

# The three ring operations on representatives.  Each is written ONCE,
# and used twice below: as the image of a generator under the object
# map, and as a slot of a model of the ring theory.
# ...and a renderer for the verdicts below: a `Bool` read as a word.
def says = ("true" | "false") ; merge

def addM = (Mod7(a) Mod7(b) -> a b ; + ; reduce)
def mulM = (Mod7(a) Mod7(b) -> a b ; * ; reduce)
def subM = (Mod7(a) Mod7(b) -> a b ; - ; reduce)
def negM = (Mod7(a) -> 0 a ; - ; reduce)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wiring goes to wiring: `dup`, `swap`, `drop` and `_` have nothing to
# declare, because their schemes never mention `Int`.  Only the words
# that do appear in the table.
model Mod in Base(Int ↦ Mod7 via reduce) =
    + = addM
    * = mulM
    - = subM

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. One polynomial, two rings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#   p(x) = x² + 3x + 2
def poly = (x -> (x x ; *) (x 3 ; *) ; + ; 2 ... ; +)
def polyMod with Mod = poly

"-- p(x) = x^2 + 3x + 2 --" ; print
5 ; poly ; print                                      # 25 + 15 + 2 = 42
5 ; reduce ; polyMod ; unMod7 ; print                 # 42 = 6*7 + 0, so 0
9 ; poly ; print                                      # 81 + 27 + 2 = 110
9 ; reduce ; polyMod ; unMod7 ; print                 # 110 = 15*7 + 5, so 5

# THE LITERALS WENT THROUGH `reduce` TOO, and here that changed nothing
# because 3 and 2 are already canonical.  Section 7 is the case where it
# changes everything.  By hand at x = 9: reduce 9 = 2, and
# 2*2 = 4, 2*3 = 6, 4 + 6 = 10 = 3, 3 + 2 = 5.

#   polyMod : Mod7 =Mod> Mod7 — the RECEIPT, one label per model,
#   exactly as a family's application leaves one.

# APPLYING IT TWICE IS THE IDENTITY THE SECOND TIME.  After `with Mod`
# there is no `Int` left in the type, so no generator mentions the
# mapped object and the functor has nothing to do.  The label set is
# idempotent for the same reason a set is.
def polyModMod with Mod = polyMod
9 ; reduce ; polyModMod ; unMod7 ; print              # 5, and one `=Mod>`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Transitive unfolding
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `poly` is not a generator with an image: it is a DEF, so the functor
# enters it.  So is everything `poly` calls.  Each is transported once
# and cached as a generated word `Mod@<def>`, which is the compiler's
# spelling (`:doc Mod@cube` shows it; `:defs` does not list it, because
# you cannot name it).
def sq    = dup ; *
def cube  = (x -> (x ; sq) x ; *)
def cubic = (x -> (x ; cube) (x 3 ; *) ; + ; 2 ... ; +)
def cubicMod with Mod = cubic

"-- q(x) = x^3 + 3x + 2, through three defs --" ; print
2 ; cubic ; print                                     # 8 + 6 + 2 = 16
2 ; reduce ; cubicMod ; unMod7 ; print                # 16 mod 7 = 2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. What is REFUSED, and by name
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The refusal is TYPE-DIRECTED.  A word passes through untouched when
# its scheme does not mention `Int`; a word that mentions `Int` and has
# neither an image nor a body is refused where the scope is written,
# naming the word and the fix.  Uncomment either to see it:
#
#   def bad with Mod = 2 ... ; div
#     `div` has no image under model Mod: Mod maps Int ↦ Mod7, and
#     `div : Int Int ⇒ Int` mentions Int — so the functor has nothing
#     to send it to.  Add `div = …` to `model Mod`, declare a
#     retraction (`via reduce, r` with `r : Mod7 ⇒ Int`) so it is
#     derived by conjugation, or do not call it here.
#
#   def bad with Mod = 3 ... ; lt? ; (drop drop | drop drop) ; merge
#     `lt?` has no image under model Mod: … `lt? : Int Int ⇒ (Int Int
#     | Int Int)` mentions Int …
#
# Both are RIGHT to be refused: Z/7 is not an ordered ring and it is
# not a field of characteristic zero, so neither `div` nor `lt?` has a
# well-defined image.  The checker does not know that; it knows that a
# functor with no value on a generator is not a functor, which is the
# same refusal for a better reason.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Reduction as a RING HOMOMORPHISM — the claim, and its audit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# An object map makes `reduce` a functor on the AMBIENT presentation.
# That it is a homomorphism of RINGS is a further and stronger claim,
# and `transformation` is where it is made and checked — square by
# square, one per generator, exactly as for any two models of a theory.
# `sample` is declared FIRST among the slots that build an `a` out of
# nothing, because that is the one a sampled square reaches for: the
# evidence a theory offers is read in declaration order, so the theory
# says which value its audits run at by writing it first.
theory Ring(a) =
    sample  : • ⇒ a
    sample2 : • ⇒ a
    add     : a a ⇒ a
    mul     : a a ⇒ a
    neg     : a ⇒ a
    zero    : • ⇒ a
    law addComm = (sample sample2 ; add) (sample2 sample ; add) ; eq? ; verdict
    law mulComm = (sample sample2 ; mul) (sample2 sample ; mul) ; eq? ; verdict
    law distrib = ((sample sample2 ; add) sample2 ; mul) ((sample sample2 ; mul) (sample2 sample2 ; mul) ; add) ; eq? ; verdict
    law negAdd  = (sample (sample ; neg) ; add) zero ; eq? ; verdict

def negInt = (x -> 0 x ; -)

model Ints in Ring(Int) =
    sample  = 7
    sample2 = 10
    add     = +
    mul     = *
    neg     = negInt
    zero    = 0

model Mods in Ring(Mod7) =
    sample  = 7 ; reduce
    sample2 = 10 ; reduce
    add     = addM
    mul     = mulM
    neg     = negM
    zero    = 0 ; Mod7

transformation Reduce in Ints ⇒ Mods = reduce
#   `:transformations` reports every square:
#
#     sample   proved        sample2  proved
#     add      sampled       mul      sampled
#     neg      sampled       zero     sampled
#
#   The two sample squares are PROVED because `reduce` of the Int one
#   IS the Mod7 one, as free programs — `Mods@sample` is literally
#   `7 ; reduce`.  The other four are SAMPLED: they are true only by the
#   arithmetic of `mod`, and `sameCode` treats `+`, `*` and `mod` as
#   uninterpreted words.  That is the same honest boundary
#   `optimizer.braid` §3 and `laws.braid` draw.

"-- reduce is a ring homomorphism --" ; print
#   reduce(7 + 10) = reduce 17 = 3, and reduce 7 + reduce 10 = 0 + 3 = 3
(7 10 ; + ; reduce) (7 10 ; reduce reduce ; addM) ; eq? ; verdict ; says ; print   # true
#   reduce(7 * 10) = reduce 70 = 0, and reduce 7 * reduce 10 = 0 * 3 = 0
(7 10 ; * ; reduce) (7 10 ; reduce reduce ; mulM) ; eq? ; verdict ; says ; print   # true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. THE CONTRAST: an object map that is NOT a homomorphism
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Floor-halving is a perfectly good object map — every generator it
# names has an image at the substituted type, so `with Halve` elaborates
# and runs.  It is NOT a ring homomorphism, and nothing in the object
# map could have said so.
data Half = Int
def halve = (x -> x 2 ; div ; Half)
def addH  = (Half(a) Half(b) -> a b ; + ; Half)
def mulH  = (Half(a) Half(b) -> a b ; * ; Half)
def subH  = (Half(a) Half(b) -> a b ; - ; Half)
def negH  = (Half(a) -> 0 a ; - ; Half)

model Halve in Base(Int ↦ Half via halve) =
    + = addH
    * = mulH
    - = subH

def polyHalf with Halve = poly

"-- the same polynomial, halved --" ; print
9 ; halve ; unHalf ; print                            # 9 div 2 = 4

# READ THE ANSWER CAREFULLY.  It is NOT p(4) = 30: the LITERALS were
# transported too, so `3` is ⌊3/2⌋ = 1 and `2` is ⌊2/2⌋ = 1, and what
# runs is 4*4 + 4*1 + 1 = 21.  That is what a functor does — it maps
# every generator, and a literal is a generator — and it is the clearest
# possible sign that `with Halve` is a reinterpretation of the whole
# program and not a change of representation.
9 ; halve ; polyHalf ; unHalf ; print                 # 16 + 4 + 1 = 21

model Halves in Ring(Half) =
    sample  = 7 ; halve
    sample2 = 10 ; halve
    add     = addH
    mul     = mulH
    neg     = negH
    zero    = 0 ; Half

# ...and the claim, refused at the FIRST square that is false.
# Uncomment to see it:
#
#   transformation Halved in Ints ⇒ Halves = halve
#     transformation Halved: the square for slot 'add' does not commute
#     at the theory's samples — a transformation between models is a
#     homomorphism, slot by slot.
#
# By hand, at the theory's own sample 7:  ⌊(7+7)/2⌋ = 7, but
# ⌊7/2⌋ + ⌊7/2⌋ = 3 + 3 = 6.  The object map typed; the homomorphism
# did not hold; and the two are checked by two different declarations,
# which is the whole point of having both.
(7 7 ; + ; halve ; unHalf) (7 7 ; halve halve ; addH ; unHalf) ; eq? ; verdict ; says ; print   # false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. A NOMINAL source, a retraction, and the conjugated table
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `A` need not be a base type.  When it is a `data` name there are no
# literals of it at all, so `via c` is not the literal family's image —
# it is the map on values, and it earns its keep the other way: declare
# a RETRACTION beside it and every generator the table does not name is
# DERIVED by conjugation, one `r` per `A` input wire and one `c` per `A`
# output wire.
data Meters = Int
data Mm     = Int

def toMm = (Meters(m) -> m 1000 ; * ; Mm)
def toM  = (Mm(x) -> x 1000 ; div ; Meters)

# An EMPTY table, and a complete model: with a retraction there is
# nothing left to write.
model Milli in Base(Meters ↦ Mm via toMm, toM) =

def stretch = (Meters(m) -> m 2 ; * ; Meters)
def start   = 3 ; Meters
def run     = start ; stretch
def runMm with Milli = run

"-- metres, then the same program in millimetres --" ; print
run   ; unMeters ; print                              # 3 m, doubled = 6
runMm ; unMm     ; print                              # 6000

# What was derived, and how.  `Meters : Int ⇒ Meters` is a generator
# with an `A` on its OUTPUT, so its image is `Meters ; toMm`;
# `unMeters : Meters ⇒ Int` has one on its INPUT, so its image is
# `toM ; unMeters`.  `stretch` is a def, so it is unfolded around them,
# and `Milli@stretch : Mm ⇒ Mm` is the conjugate the two together make.
#
# `:doc Milli` says what is PROVED and what is not.  Here the retraction
# law `toMm ; toM = id` is TRUE — (m·1000) div 1000 = m for every Int m
# — and `sameCode` does not decide it, because `*` and `div` are
# uninterpreted words in the free category.  So `Milli` is recorded as a
# DIALECT: the conjugated words are what the declaration says they are,
# on the author's word.  That is the same line `optimizer.braid` draws
# between an optimizer (images PROVABLY equal to their generators) and a
# reinterpretation, and it is drawn here by the same procedure.
(3 ; Meters ; toMm ; toM ; unMeters) (3) ; eq? ; verdict ; says ; print   # true, by hand
